    40	
    41	## Definition
    42	
    43	**The successor cardinal.** For a cardinal $\kappa$ ([[def-cardinal]]) write
    44	
    45	$$\kappa^{+} \;:=\; \aleph(\kappa),$$
    46	
    47	the Hartogs number of $\kappa$ ([[thm-hartogs]]). By
    48	[[lem-successor-cardinal-exists]] this is the least cardinal strictly above
    49	$\kappa$, and its existence is a theorem of ZF.
    50	
    51	> **Notation rule, in force on this page and its companion.** The superscript
    52	> $+$ means the **successor cardinal** only on a cardinal letter
    53	> $\kappa, \lambda, \mu$ or an aleph. On an ordinal letter
    54	> $\alpha, \beta, \gamma, \xi, \eta$ the superscript $+$ keeps its published
    55	> meaning, the **ordinal successor** $\alpha^{+} = \alpha \cup \{\alpha\}$ of
    56	> [[def-ordinal]]. The two never agree on an infinite cardinal: $\kappa \cup \{\kappa\}$
    57	> is a successor ordinal and therefore not a cardinal at all, while $\kappa^{+}$
    58	> is much larger. To keep the reader out of the collision, everything below
    59	> writes $\alpha + 1$ for the ordinal successor and reserves $\kappa^{+}$ for the
    60	> cardinal one.
    61	
    62	**The alephs.** By [[cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined]] there
    63	is exactly one operation $\alpha \mapsto \aleph_\alpha$, defined at every ordinal
    64	([[def-ordinal]]), with
    65	
    66	$$\aleph_0 = \omega, \qquad \aleph_{\alpha+1} = \aleph_\alpha^{+}, \qquad \aleph_\lambda = \sup\{\, \aleph_\alpha : \alpha \in \lambda \,\} \ \ (\lambda \text{ a limit ordinal}),$$
    67	
    68	the limit clause being taken over limit ordinals in the sense of
    69	[[def-limit-ordinal]], and $\sup$ being $\bigcup$ applied to a set of ordinals.
    70	Every $\aleph_\alpha$ is an infinite cardinal, the operation is strictly
    71	increasing, and $\alpha \le \aleph_\alpha$; all of this is that corollary, and all
    72	of it is ZF.
    73	
    74	**The beths**, assuming the Axiom of Choice, are the parallel operation
    75	
    76	$$\beth_0 = \omega, \qquad \beth_{\alpha+1} = 2^{\beth_\alpha}, \qquad \beth_\lambda = \sup\{\, \beth_\alpha : \alpha \in \lambda \,\} \ \ (\lambda \text{ a limit ordinal}),$$
    77	
    78	with $2^{\kappa}$ the cardinal power of [[def-cardinal-arithmetic]].
    79	
    80	**Successor and limit cardinals.** An infinite cardinal $\kappa$ is a
    81	**successor cardinal** when $\kappa = \lambda^{+}$ for some infinite cardinal
    82	$\lambda$, and a **limit cardinal** otherwise. So $\aleph_{\alpha+1}$ is a
    83	successor cardinal for every $\alpha$, and $\aleph_0 = \omega$ is a limit
    84	cardinal, there being no infinite cardinal below it.
    85	
    86	**The two identifications.**
    87	
    88	$$\aleph_0 = \omega, \qquad \aleph_1 = \omega_1 .$$
    89	
    90	The first is the base clause. The second holds because
    91	$\aleph_1 = \aleph_0^{+} = \aleph(\omega)$, and $\aleph(\omega)$ is by definition
    92	the first uncountable ordinal $\omega_1$ ([[def-first-uncountable-ordinal]]);
    93	[[thm-omega-one-is-the-least-uncountable-ordinal]] independently confirms that
    94	$\omega_1$ is a cardinal, is uncountable ([[def-countable]]), and has every
    95	ordinal below it at most countable, which is the same thing said in the language
    96	of the ordinal development.
    97	
    98	## Remarks
    99	
   100	**Why the aleph notation is introduced at all, when $\omega$ and $\omega_1$
   101	already exist.** The subscript is an *ordinal index into the cardinals*: it makes
   102	"the $\alpha$-th infinite cardinal" a term of the language, so that statements
   103	such as "$\aleph_{\alpha+1}$ is regular" and "$\aleph_\omega$ is singular" can be
   104	quantified over $\alpha$. The published ordinal development deliberately avoids
   105	the notation, writing $\omega$ and $\omega_1$ throughout, precisely so that an
   106	ordinal computation there is never silently read as a cardinal one; that
   107	convention is recorded in [[rem-ordinal-versus-cardinal-exponentiation]] and is
   108	not disturbed here. On this page the reverse convention is in force: an aleph is
   109	always a cardinal, and an exponential is always the cardinal one.
   110	
   111	**$\aleph$ with an argument and $\aleph$ with a subscript are different things.**
   112	$\aleph(A)$ is the Hartogs number of a **set** $A$, defined for every set in ZF
   113	([[thm-hartogs]]); $\aleph_\alpha$ is the $\alpha$-th infinite cardinal. They
   114	agree in the one case that matters, $\aleph(\aleph_\alpha) = \aleph_{\alpha+1}$,
   115	which is the successor clause, and the same symbol is used because the notation
   116	is Hartogs' own.
   117	
   118	**Where the beths sit.** $\beth_0 = \aleph_0$, by the two base clauses. The two
   119	hierarchies then climb by different rules: the aleph step takes the **least**
   120	cardinal strictly above, and the beth step takes the power. Whether they
   121	nevertheless agree at every index is the generalised continuum hypothesis, which
   122	is not decided by the axioms in use here and is asserted nowhere on this page or
   123	its companion.
