    31	---
    32	
    33	## Definition
    34	
    35	Let $\alpha$ be an ordinal ([[def-ordinal]]).
    36	
    37	- $\alpha$ is a **successor ordinal** when $\alpha = \beta^{+} = \beta \cup \{\beta\}$
    38	  for some ordinal $\beta$, which is then an ordinal by [[lem-ordinal-basics]];
    39	- $\alpha$ is a **limit ordinal** when $\alpha \ne 0$ and $\alpha$ is not a
    40	  successor ordinal.
    41	
    42	Every ordinal is therefore exactly one of: $0$, a successor ordinal, or a limit
    43	ordinal. The three cases are the three clauses of a definition or a proof by
    44	transfinite recursion or induction over the ordinals.
    45	
    46	## Remarks
    47	
    48	- **The predecessor of a successor is unique.** If $\alpha = \beta^{+}$ then
    49	  $\beta \in \alpha$ and every $\xi \in \alpha$ satisfies $\xi \le \beta$, so
    50	  $\beta$ is the largest element of $\alpha$ and is determined by $\alpha$. In
    51	  particular $\beta^{+} = \gamma^{+}$ forces $\beta = \gamma$.
    52	- **Union characterisation.** For a nonzero ordinal $\alpha$: $\alpha$ is a limit
    53	  ordinal if and only if $\alpha = \bigcup \alpha$, and $\alpha$ is a successor
    54	  if and only if $\bigcup \alpha \in \alpha$, in which case
    55	  $\alpha = (\bigcup \alpha)^{+}$. For the successor case,
    56	  $\bigcup(\beta \cup \{\beta\}) = (\bigcup \beta) \cup \beta = \beta$, because
    57	  $\bigcup \beta \subseteq \beta$ by transitivity. For the limit case,
    58	  $\bigcup \alpha \subseteq \alpha$ always holds by transitivity, and
    59	  conversely, given $\xi \in \alpha$, the ordinal $\xi^{+}$ satisfies
    60	  $\xi^{+} \in \alpha$: by [[lem-ordinal-trichotomy]] the alternatives are
    61	  $\xi^{+} = \alpha$, excluded because $\alpha$ is not a successor, and
    62	  $\alpha \in \xi^{+}$, which gives $\alpha \in \xi$ or $\alpha = \xi$ and hence
    63	  $\alpha \in \alpha$ using $\xi \in \alpha$ and transitivity, excluded by
    64	  [[lem-ordinal-basics]]; so $\xi \in \xi^{+} \in \alpha$ puts
    65	  $\xi \in \bigcup \alpha$. The hypothesis $\alpha \ne 0$ cannot be dropped,
    66	  since $\bigcup 0 = 0$.
    67	- **Closure under successor.** The previous paragraph says exactly that a nonzero
    68	  ordinal is a limit if and only if it is closed under the successor operation.
    69	  That is the form in which limit ordinals are recognised in practice.
    70	- $0$ is not a limit ordinal here. Some texts include it, so that "limit
    71	  ordinal" means "$\alpha = \bigcup \alpha$" outright. The convention adopted is
    72	  the more widely used one, and it is the one that makes "$0$, successor, limit"
    73	  a genuine three way split.
    74	- The least limit ordinal is $\omega$ ([[lem-omega-least-limit-ordinal]]), so the
    75	  distinction is invisible below $\omega$: every natural number is either $0$ or
    76	  a successor. That is precisely why ordinary induction on $\mathbb{N}$ needs
    77	  only a base case and a successor step, while induction over the ordinals needs
    78	  a limit clause as well.
