    38	
    39	## Definition
    40	
    41	Let $\alpha$ be an ordinal ([[def-ordinal]]). The **cofinality** of $\alpha$ is
    42	
    43	$$\operatorname{cf}(\alpha) \;:=\; \text{the least ordinal } \beta \text{ for which some } f : \beta \to \alpha \text{ has cofinal range},$$
    44	
    45	cofinal range meaning that $f[\beta]$ is a cofinal subset of $\alpha$
    46	([[def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal]]): every $\zeta \in \alpha$ satisfies
    47	$\zeta \le f(\xi)$ for some $\xi \in \beta$. That such a least ordinal exists, and
    48	that a witnessing map of that length may be taken strictly increasing, is
    49	[[lem-cofinality-is-well-defined]], and both are theorems of ZF. So
    50	$\operatorname{cf}$ is defined at every ordinal, without any choice principle.
    51	
    52	**Regular and singular.** An **infinite cardinal** $\kappa$
    53	([[def-cardinal]], [[def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies]]) is
    54	
    55	- **regular** when $\operatorname{cf}(\kappa) = \kappa$;
    56	- **singular** when $\operatorname{cf}(\kappa) \ne \kappa$.
    57	
    58	The two cases are exhaustive by definition, and by
    59	[[thm-cofinality-basics]] singular means exactly
    60	$\operatorname{cf}(\kappa) < \kappa$, since $\operatorname{cf}(\alpha) \le \alpha$
    61	always holds.
    62	
    63	## Remarks
    64	
    65	**Why regularity is defined for cardinals and not for ordinals.** The definition
    66	of $\operatorname{cf}$ applies to every ordinal, and it must, because the
    67	construction quantifies over maps into $\alpha$ of every length. But
    68	$\operatorname{cf}(\alpha) = \alpha$ is an uninteresting condition on a general
    69	ordinal: it fails at $\omega + 1$ and at $\omega \cdot 2$ for reasons that have
    70	nothing to do with size, and it holds only at $0$, at $1$, and at those ordinals
    71	that are already infinite cardinals. Calling an ordinal regular would therefore
    72	say nothing new, which is why the words are attached to cardinals here.
    73	
    74	**What a singular cardinal is, in one sentence.** A cardinal that is reachable
    75	from below by fewer than $\kappa$ steps: there is a strictly increasing family of
    76	ordinals below $\kappa$, indexed by an ordinal strictly shorter than $\kappa$,
    77	whose supremum is $\kappa$. That is exactly the failure of regularity, and
    78	[[thm-regularity-of-the-alephs]] exhibits a cardinal for which it happens.
    79	
    80	**Why $\operatorname{cf}(\kappa)$ being a regular cardinal is a theorem and not
    81	part of the definition.** Regularity is *defined through* $\operatorname{cf}$, so
    82	building "$\operatorname{cf}(\kappa)$ is regular" into the definition would make
    83	the definition refer to itself. The statement is true, and it is
    84	[[thm-cofinality-basics]]; it is recorded here as the item that discharges the
    85	naming obligation of this definition, and nothing above depends on it.
    86	
    87	**Only one notion of "cofinal" exists in this library.**
    88	[[def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal]] introduces cofinal subsets, because the
    89	boundedness theorem for $\omega_1$ needs them, and deliberately introduces
    90	neither the cofinality function nor the regular/singular vocabulary. Both are
    91	introduced here, and the definition above is written in exactly that item's
    92	terms, so no second notion is created.
