    32	
    33	## Definition
    34	
    35	Let $\alpha$ be an ordinal ([[def-ordinal]]). A subset $C \subseteq \alpha$ is
    36	**cofinal in $\alpha$**, equivalently **unbounded in $\alpha$**, when
    37	
    38	$$\text{for every } \xi \in \alpha \text{ there is } \eta \in C \text{ with } \xi \le \eta.$$
    39	
    40	A subset that is not cofinal is **bounded below $\alpha$**: there is
    41	$\xi \in \alpha$ such that $\eta < \xi$ for every $\eta \in C$.
    42	
    43	## Remarks
    44	
    45	- **At a limit ordinal, cofinal means the supremum is attained from below.** If
    46	  $\lambda$ is a limit ordinal ([[def-limit-ordinal]]) and $C \subseteq \lambda$
    47	  is nonempty, then $C$ is cofinal in $\lambda$ if and only if
    48	  $\sup C = \bigcup C = \lambda$ (claim (e) of [[lem-ordinal-basics]]). If
    49	  $\bigcup C = \lambda$, then every $\xi \in \lambda$ lies in some $\eta \in C$,
    50	  so $\xi < \eta$ and $C$ is cofinal. Conversely, if $C$ is cofinal then
    51	  $\bigcup C \subseteq \lambda$, because each $\eta \in C$ satisfies
    52	  $\eta \subseteq \lambda$ by transitivity; and for $\xi \in \lambda$ the ordinal
    53	  $\xi^{+}$ again lies in $\lambda$ ([[def-limit-ordinal]]), so cofinality supplies
    54	  $\eta \in C$ with $\xi^{+} \le \eta$, whence $\xi \in \xi^{+} \subseteq \eta$ and
    55	  $\xi \in \bigcup C$, giving $\lambda \subseteq \bigcup C$. This is the form in which the
    56	  notion is used on this page, and it is exactly the hypothesis of the
    57	  continuity clause of [[thm-ordinal-arithmetic-monotonicity]].
    58	
    59	- **At $0$ and at successors the notion is degenerate.** $\varnothing$ is
    60	  cofinal in $0$, vacuously, and it is the only subset of $0$. If
    61	  $\alpha = \delta^{+}$ then $\delta$ is the greatest element of $\alpha$
    62	  ([[lem-ordinal-basics]], [[lem-ordinal-trichotomy]]), so a subset is cofinal
    63	  in $\alpha$ if and only if it contains $\delta$. The interesting case is the
    64	  limit case, and that is where the notion is used.
    65	
    66	- **What is not defined at this point in the reading order.** The **cofinality**
    67	  $\operatorname{cf}(\alpha)$, the least order type of a cofinal subset, and the
    68	  vocabulary of **regular** and **singular** cardinals, are not introduced here;
    69	  they are introduced later, on Cardinal Arithmetic, Cofinality and the Alephs.
    70	  Nothing on this page needs them: the boundedness theorem below is stated as
    71	  "no at most countable subset is cofinal", which is a statement about subsets
    72	  and not about a cardinal invariant.
    73	
    74	- **Cofinal is a property of the pair, not of the set.** $\omega$ is cofinal in
    75	  $\omega$ and bounded below $\omega + 1$. The ordinal must always be named.
