    40	---
    41	
    42	## Statement
    43	
    44	Call a set $X$ **well-orderable** when some relation well-orders it
    45	([[def-well-order]]). Work in ZF, with no choice principle. Then:
    46	
    47	**(a)** $X$ is well-orderable if and only if $X \approx \alpha$
    48	([[def-equinumerous]]) for some ordinal $\alpha$ ([[def-ordinal]]).
    49	
    50	**(b)** If $X$ is well-orderable there is a **least** ordinal equinumerous with
    51	$X$. It is written $\lvert X \rvert$ and called the **cardinality** of $X$.
    52	
    53	**(c)** $\lvert X \rvert$ is a cardinal ([[def-cardinal]]).
    54	
    55	**(d)** If $X \approx Y$ and $X$ is well-orderable, then $Y$ is well-orderable
    56	and $\lvert Y \rvert = \lvert X \rvert$.
    57	
    58	**(e)** $\lvert \alpha \rvert \le \alpha$ for every ordinal $\alpha$, and
    59	$\lvert \alpha \rvert = \alpha$ exactly when $\alpha$ is a cardinal.
    60	
    61	Assuming the Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]) every set is
    62	well-orderable ([[thm-well-ordering-theorem]]), so $\lvert X \rvert$ is then
    63	defined for every set and is exactly the cardinality of [[def-cardinal]].
    64	
    65	**Why this item exists.** [[def-cardinal]] introduces $\lvert X \rvert$ under the
    66	hypothesis "Assume the Axiom of Choice", and it needs that hypothesis only to
    67	know that $X$ carries a well-order at all. Everything below is about
    68	well-orderable sets and is a theorem of ZF, which is what makes it possible to
    69	state Hessenberg's theorem and Tarski's theorem, one of which is choice-free and
    70	the other of which is precisely about the gap between ZF and ZFC.
    71	
    72	## Facts & Assumptions
    73	
    74	**Given:** The axioms of ZF, in particular Separation and Replacement. No choice principle is assumed except where the Axiom of Choice is named.
    75	
    76	[L1] Every well-order is order isomorphic to exactly one ordinal, its order type ([[thm-mostowski-collapse]]).
    77	
    78	[L2] An order isomorphism is in particular a bijection ([[def-order-isomorphism]], [[def-injection-surjection-bijection]]).
    79	
    80	[L3] $\alpha^{+} = \alpha \cup \{\alpha\}$ is an ordinal, every element of an ordinal is an ordinal, $\alpha \notin \alpha$, and $\alpha \subseteq \beta$ if and only if $\alpha \in \beta$ or $\alpha = \beta$ ([[lem-ordinal-basics]], [[def-ordinal]]).
    81	
    82	[L4] For ordinals exactly one of $\alpha \in \beta$, $\alpha = \beta$, $\beta \in \alpha$ holds, and every nonempty set of ordinals has an $\in$-least element ([[lem-ordinal-trichotomy]]).
    83	
    84	[L5] $\approx$ is reflexive, symmetric and transitive, and the order relation $\alpha \le \beta$ on ordinals is $\alpha \subseteq \beta$ ([[def-equinumerous]], [[def-ordinal]]).
    85	
    86	[L6] An ordinal $\kappa$ is a **cardinal** when no $\alpha \in \kappa$ satisfies $\alpha \approx \kappa$; under the Axiom of Choice, $\lvert X \rvert$ is the least ordinal equinumerous with $X$ ([[def-cardinal]]).
    87	
    88	[L7] Assuming the Axiom of Choice, every set carries a well-order ([[def-axiom-of-choice]], [[thm-well-ordering-theorem]]).
    89	
    90	## Proof
    91	
    92	**Proof technique:** direct.
    93	
    94	1.1 If $<$ well-orders $X$ then $(X, <)$ has an order type $\alpha$ and the collapsing map is an order isomorphism, hence a bijection $X \to \alpha$, so $X \approx \alpha$. [L1, L2]
    95	
    96	1.2 Conversely, if $f : X \to \alpha$ is a bijection then $x <_X y :\iff f(x) \in f(y)$ is a well-order of $X$, since $f$ transports irreflexivity, transitivity, trichotomy and the least-element property of $\in$ on $\alpha$ back to $X$; this proves claim (a). [L2, L3, L4]
    97	
    98	1.3 Assume now $X \approx \alpha$ for an ordinal $\alpha$, and put $C = \{\xi \in \alpha^{+} : \xi \approx X\}$, a set by Separation inside the ordinal $\alpha^{+}$, all of whose elements are ordinals, and nonempty because $\alpha \in \alpha^{+}$ and $\alpha \approx X$. [L3, L5]
    99	
   100	2.1 By [L4] the set $C$ has an $\in$-least element $\kappa$, and $\kappa \approx X$. [step 1.3, L4]
   101	
   102	3.1 $\kappa$ is least among **all** ordinals equinumerous with $X$: given $\beta \approx X$, trichotomy gives $\beta \in \alpha^{+}$, in which case $\beta \in C$ and $\kappa \subseteq \beta$ by minimality; or else $\alpha^{+} \subseteq \beta$, in which case $\alpha \in \alpha^{+} \subseteq \beta$ gives $\alpha \subseteq \beta$, while $\alpha \in C$ gives $\kappa \subseteq \alpha$, so again $\kappa \subseteq \beta$. This proves claim (b), with $\lvert X \rvert := \kappa$. [step 2.1, L3, L4, L5]
   103	
   104	4.1 Claim (c): if $\gamma \in \kappa$ had $\gamma \approx \kappa$ then $\gamma \approx X$ by [L5], so $\kappa \subseteq \gamma$ by step 3.1, whence $\gamma \in \kappa \subseteq \gamma$ and $\gamma \in \gamma$, which [L3] forbids; so $\kappa$ is a cardinal. [step 3.1, L3, L5, L6]
   105	
   106	4.2 Claim (d): if $X \approx Y$ then an ordinal is equinumerous with $X$ exactly when it is equinumerous with $Y$, by symmetry and transitivity of $\approx$, so the two least such ordinals coincide; and $Y \approx \kappa$ makes $Y$ well-orderable by step 1.2. [step 3.1, step 1.2, L5]
   107	
   108	5.1 Claim (e): $\alpha \approx \alpha$, so $\lvert \alpha \rvert \subseteq \alpha$ by step 3.1; if $\alpha$ is a cardinal then no $\xi \in \alpha$ is equinumerous with $\alpha$, so the least ordinal equinumerous with $\alpha$ is $\alpha$ itself; and conversely $\lvert \alpha \rvert = \alpha$ makes $\alpha$ a cardinal by step 4.1. [step 3.1, step 4.1, L5, L6]
   109	
   110	6.1 Assuming the Axiom of Choice, every set $X$ carries a well-order by [L7], hence $X \approx \lvert X \rvert$ by step 1.1 and $\lvert X \rvert$ is defined for every set; and by step 3.1 it is the least ordinal equinumerous with $X$, which is what [L6] calls the cardinality of $X$. [step 1.1, step 3.1, L6, L7] ∎
   111	
   112	## Remarks
   113	
   114	**What is choice-free and what is not.** Claims (a) to (e) are theorems of ZF: they say what happens *for a well-orderable set*, and the hypothesis of well-orderability is carried explicitly rather than supplied by an axiom. The Axiom of Choice enters only in the last step, where it removes the hypothesis by making every set well-orderable. Without choice a set may be equinumerous with no ordinal at all, and then $\lvert X \rvert$ simply does not exist; that is the situation [[thm-hartogs]] is designed for.
   115	
   116	**Nothing is chosen.** The one place a selection might be expected is step 2.1, and there the element taken is the $\in$-**least** member of $C$, which is determined by $C$ and not selected from it. Step 3.1 then shows that the bound $\alpha^{+}$, which exists only to turn "the least ordinal equinumerous with $X$" into an instance of Separation over a set, does not affect the answer.
   117	
   118	**Notation.** From here on $\lvert X \rvert$ always means the ordinal of claim (b). For a finite set this is not yet known to agree with the natural number written $\lvert A \rvert$ in [[def-finite-cardinality]]; that agreement is a theorem and is proved on this page.
